  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Transportation obligated $9.6B in federal contract spending for FY2026 to date. The Department awarded 11,621 contracts, with an average award size of $826K, and obligations are up 6.4% year over year. Engineering Services is the largest NAICS category with $1.4B in obligations. The Department purchased systems and hardware classified under Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $1B, and Telecommunications Resellers at $922M. The Department also obligated significant dollars for infrastructure and logistics, including Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $688M and Deep Sea Freight Transportation at $584M. Vendors with the largest totals include HARRIS CORPORATION at $922M and RAYTHEON COMPANY at $648M. The top ten vendors together reflect major spending on communications, avionics, and systems integration. The FY26 to date total of $9.6B follows a trend from prior years: FY21 $7B, FY22 $8B, FY23 $8.7B, FY24 $8.6B, and FY25 $9B. Current-year totals are partial. All figures are contract obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Transportation's contract spending trended year over year?",
        "body": "The Department of Transportation obligated $9.6B for FY26 to date, up 6.4% year over year. FY26 follows a sequence of fiscal-year obligations: FY21 $7B, FY22 $8B, FY23 $8.7B, FY24 $8.6B, and FY25 $9B. The average award size for FY26 to date is $826K across 11,621 awards. Current-year totals are partial and reported as fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the biggest categories and vendors in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Engineering Services leads NAICS categories at $1.4B, followed by Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $1B and Telecommunications Resellers at $922M. The Department also obligated funds in Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $688M and Deep Sea Freight Transportation at $584M. HARRIS CORPORATION is the top vendor at $922M, followed by RAYTHEON COMPANY at $648M and ROHDE & SCHWARZ USA, INC. at $403M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Transportation spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Transportation obligated $9.6B in contracts for FY2026 to date. The Department made 11,621 awards for FY26 to date with an average award size of $826K, and obligations are up 6.4% year over year. Current-year totals are partial and reported fiscal year to date."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Transportation buy?",
      "a": "The Department of Transportation buys engineering and technical services and systems. Engineering Services received $1.4B, Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing received $1B, Telecommunications Resellers received $922M, Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction received $688M, and Deep Sea Freight Transportation received $584M. The Department also obligated funds to Telephone Apparatus Manufacturing, Computer Systems Design Services, and computing infrastructure providers across the top ten NAICS categories."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Transportation's largest contractors?",
      "a": "HARRIS CORPORATION, RAYTHEON COMPANY, and ROHDE & SCHWARZ USA, INC. are the largest contractors in FY2026 to date by obligations. HARRIS CORPORATION received $922M, RAYTHEON COMPANY received $648M, and ROHDE & SCHWARZ USA, INC. received $403M. Other top vendors include INDRA AIR TRAFFIC, INC. at $377M, PTSI MANAGED SERVICES INC at $304M, and TOTE SERVICES, LLC at $215M."
